The  network  interface pseudo device encapsulates datagrams into
IP.  These encapsulated datagrams are  routed  to  a  destination
host, where they are decapsulated and further routed to their fi‐
nal  destination.  The appears to the inner datagrams as one hop.
interfaces are dynamically created and  destroyed  with  the  and
subcommands.   This driver currently supports the following modes
of operation: Encapsulated datagrams are prepended an outer data‐
gram and a GRE header.  The GRE header specifies the type of  the
encapsulated  datagram and thus allows for tunneling other proto‐
cols than IP like e.g. AppleTalk.  GRE mode is also  the  default
tunnel  mode  on Cisco routers.  This is also the default mode of
operation  of  the  interfaces.    Encapsulated   datagrams   are
prepended a GRE header, and then they are sent over a UDP socket.
Userland  may create the socket and it to the kernel using the If
userland does not supply a socket, then the  kernel  will  create
one  using  the  addresses and ports supplied by and/or Datagrams
are encapsulated into IP, but with a shorter encapsulation.   The
original IP header is modified and the modifications are inserted
between  the  so  modified header and the original payload.  Like
only for IP in IP encapsulation.  The interfaces support a number
of such as: Set the IP address of the local tunnel end.  This  is
the source address set by or displayed by ifconfig for the inter‐
face.   Set the IP address of the remote tunnel end.  This is the
destination address set by or displayed by ifconfig for  the  in‐
terface.   Query  the IP address that is set for the local tunnel
end.  This is the address the encapsulation header carries as lo‐
