tunnel: On Router A:
   # ifconfig greN create
   # ifconfig greN 192.168.1.1 192.168.2.1
   # ifconfig greN tunnel A B
   # route add ‐net 192.168.2 ‐netmask 255.255.255.0  192.168.2.1
On Router B:
   # ifconfig greN create
   # ifconfig greN 192.168.2.1 192.168.1.1
   # ifconfig greN tunnel B A
   #  route add ‐net 192.168.1 ‐netmask 255.255.255.0 192.168.1.1
To setup the same tunnel as above, but using GRE in UDP  encapsu‐
lation  instead  of  GRE encapsulation, set flags and and specify
source and destination UDP ports.  On Router A:
   # ifconfig greN create
   # ifconfig greN link0 link2
   # ifconfig greN 192.168.1.1 192.168.2.1
   # ifconfig greN tunnel A,port‐A B,port‐B
   # route add ‐net 192.168.2 ‐netmask 255.255.255.0  192.168.2.1
On Router B:
   # ifconfig greN create
   # ifconfig greN link0 link2
   # ifconfig greN 192.168.2.1 192.168.1.1
   # ifconfig greN tunnel B,port‐B A,port‐A
   #  route add ‐net 192.168.1 ‐netmask 255.255.255.0 192.168.1.1
